   "claims": [
    "Question or Feedback Name (optional) Email (optional) Results: Body Mass Index (BMI) BMI Classification Weight Ranges:[1] Weight ranges for each classification based on height.",
    "The formula for BMI is: BMI = weight [kg] / height² [m²] Imperial Units BMI = weight [lb] / height² [in²] × 703 Or simply use the calculator above and it will do the work for you!",
    "For instance, a person with a BMI above 35 or 40 is more likely obese.",
    "As an example, a 7-year-old boy with a BMI of 20 would fall into the greater than 95th percentile and would be considered obese.",
    "However, a 15-year-old boy with a BMI of 20 would fall between the 25th and 50th percentile and would be considered to be a healthy weight.",
    "These BMI charts can be accessed through the CDC website (boys[5] and girls[6]).",
    "However, a 2016 study found that using a target BMI in the IBW equation gives a more accurate representation of ideal body weight.",
    "Studies have found that a BMI around 22 confers the lowest morbidity in both men and women.[11] So using this formula, a 5′ 5″ woman targeting a healthy BMI of 22 would have an ideal body weight of 132 pounds.",
    "B., Universal equation for estimating ideal body weight and body weight at any BMI, The American journal of clinical nutrition, 2016, 103(5), 1197-1203."
   ],
